# =============================================================================
# meanul-hailpath — MASTER SETTINGS FILE
# =============================================================================
# Copy to .env and edit:   make init   (or: cp .env.example .env)
#
# This one file configures the whole stack. The root docker-compose.yaml
# includes every component without an `env_file:`, so all fourteen of them
# resolve their ${...} from here. The per-component .env.example files stay
# as the reference for what each component consumes, and are what a
# standalone single-component run uses; they play no part in the full stack.
#
# THE ONLY THING YOU MUST EDIT IS SECTION 1. Everything below it has a
# working default and can be left alone on a first deployment.
#
# Values may reference values defined ABOVE them (${LIKE_THIS}), which is how
# the three names PostgreSQL's password goes by stay one secret.
#
# `make preflight` refuses to deploy while any change-me placeholder is left.
# =============================================================================


# =============================================================================
# 1. SECRETS — CHANGE EVERY ONE OF THESE
# =============================================================================
# Nothing here has a safe default on purpose: the compose files use
# ${VAR:?...} so a missing value fails immediately instead of starting
# with a password somebody could guess.

# PostgreSQL superuser. Created on first start, then used by bootstrap,
# the services, and Debezium.
PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD=change-me

# Redis. One password, used by every service.
REDIS_PASSWORD=change-me-redis

# ClickHouse. Created by the image on first start; Grafana, bootstrap and
# the sink all log in with it.
CH_PASSWORD=change-me-clickhouse

# ksqlDB's HTTP Basic auth. This is how a SQL client reads Kafka.
# Must not contain ':' or ',': the credential is stored as `user: password,role`.
KSQLDB_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-ksqldb

# Kafka SASL PLAIN (one broker, SASL_SSL). TLS keystore password is local.
KAFKA_SASL_PASSWORD=change-me-kafka
KAFKA_SSL_STORE_PASSWORD=change-me-kafka-store

# Grafana admin login.
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-grafana

# ---- derived from the above; do not edit ------------------------------------
# PostgreSQL's password reaches its three consumers under three different
# names. They must be the same secret, so they are written once above and
# referenced here rather than repeated.
PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}
CDC_PG_PASSWORD=${PG_SUPERUSER_PASSWORD}


# =============================================================================
# 2. STACK-WIDE
# =============================================================================
# Every container runs UTC, and every stored timestamp is UTC.
TZ=UTC

# How the generators decide "what hour is it". Stored timestamps stay UTC
# (TZ above). Demand weights, TLC calibration, and day_period (night/
# morning/afternoon/evening) are indexed in this zone so NYC rush hour
# lands on NYC rush hour, not UTC's.
SIM_TIMEZONE=America/New_York

# How much the Python components say. DEBUG on the whole stack is a lot;
# prefer restarting one container with it.
LOG_LEVEL=INFO


# Which network the IMAGE BUILDS use. Defaults to the host's own network: a
# build stage's traffic is forwarded through the bridge, which is exactly
# what a route in front of this host often cannot see, even while the
# host's own connections work fine. The give-away, if this were the wrong
# setting: `docker pull` works (the daemon is a host process) while the
# first `apt-get` inside a build is refused.
#
# It has no effect on how the stack runs: every container still joins the
# hailpath-net bridge either way, regardless of this value.
#
# Set to `default` if this host has no such gap — a plain deployment with
# nothing in front of it, for instance.
BUILD_NETWORK=host


# =============================================================================
# 3. WHERE THE DATA LIVES
# =============================================================================
# Each store's data directory is a bind mount under this root, instead of
# being left in Docker's own volume area. That keeps the databases, the
# topics, the warehouse and the downloaded street map on whichever disk you
# want them on, without touching the Docker daemon's configuration.
#
# Point it at the largest, fastest disk you have. Images stay wherever the
# Docker daemon's data-root is; only the data comes here. That split is
# deliberate: the images are ~15 GB and static, whereas ClickHouse grows
# 1-2 GB a day and PostgreSQL and Kafka want the faster spindle.
#
# `make init` creates the tree. `make preflight` refuses to deploy if the
# root is missing or not writable.
#
# Left as a /change-me placeholder, because unlike every other setting here
# there is no
# value that is even plausible on two different machines: this used to ship
# /data-root/lssd/hailpath, which exists on the deploy host and nowhere
# else, so `make init` died on `cannot create` on any other machine before
# it had finished writing .env. `make init` detects a writable default and
# tells you what it picked. On the deploy host it is
# /data-root/lssd/hailpath.
#
# The leading slash is not decoration: this value is interpolated straight
# into a bind-mount source, and a placeholder without one is read by compose
# as the NAME of a volume that does not exist ("refers to undefined volume").
#
# ONE THING TO KNOW: because these are bind mounts, `docker volume rm` and
# `docker compose down -v` remove the volume ENTRY but leave the files on
# disk. `make destroy` and `make clean` therefore delete this tree
# explicitly — nothing else does, and a stale tree would be found by the
# next bring-up as if it were fresh.
HAILPATH_VOLUME_ROOT=/change-me


# =============================================================================
# 4. HOST PORTS (lab). Each store publishes its own.
# =============================================================================
# Change these if something on the host already holds one — `make preflight`
# tells you which.
#
# Lab: each store publishes its own port for DBeaver and `make urls`.
# Production would publish Grafana (3000) only.

PROXY_PG_PORT=5432
PROXY_REDIS_PORT=6379
PROXY_KSQLDB_PORT=8089
PROXY_DEBEZIUM_PORT=8083
PROXY_CH_HTTP_PORT=8123
PROXY_CH_NATIVE_PORT=9000
PROXY_GRAFANA_PORT=3000
PROXY_TILES_PORT=8080

# Kafka's advertised port 9094 is a fixed literal in the root
# docker-compose.yaml, not a variable. See the comment next to it.


# =============================================================================
# 5. IMAGES (all pinned to an exact version)
# =============================================================================
# `make pull` fetches every one of these and is the first thing to run on a
# new host: a tag that has moved or been withdrawn should surface then, not
# halfway through a bring-up.
#
# Every tag here names an exact release. A floating tag like `redis:8.2` or
# `python:3.13-slim` would mean two builds months apart quietly produce
# different images, which is the opposite of what this repository is for.

# The version stamped on the ten images this repository builds. It is the
# STACK's version, not any base image's — which base each one was built on is
# recorded in its org.opencontainers.image.base.name label, so:
#   docker image inspect hailpath/driver:0.1.0 \
#     -f '{{index .Config.Labels "org.opencontainers.image.base.name"}}'
HAILPATH_VERSION=0.1.0

# Python components (h-bootstrap and the services). Patch-pinned, not
# python:3.13-slim — that tag moves. uv is copied out of this image as a
# single binary; it is never `pip install uv`.
PYTHON_IMAGE=python:3.13.18-slim
UV_IMAGE=ghcr.io/astral-sh/uv:0.13.9

# a-infra-postgres. The pgRouting project's image: Postgres 18,
# PostGIS 3.6, and pgRouting 3.8. Pulled, not built.
PG_IMAGE=pgrouting/pgrouting:18-3.6-3.8.0

# b-infra-redis.
REDIS_IMAGE=redis:8.10.3

# c-infra-kafka. The Schema Registry, ksqlDB, and the Avro converter
# Debezium uses are Confluent components and must stay on the same major line.
KAFKA_IMAGE=apache/kafka:4.3.2
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_IMAGE=confluentinc/cp-schema-registry:8.3.3
KSQLDB_IMAGE=confluentinc/cp-ksqldb-server:8.3.3
AVRO_CONVERTER_VERSION=8.3.3
BUSYBOX_IMAGE=busybox:1.38.0

# d-infra-debezium.
CONNECT_IMAGE=quay.io/debezium/connect:3.6.4.Final

# e-infra-clickhouse. 26.8 is the current Long-Term Support line —
# ClickHouse marks one in March and one in August each year and supports it
# for twelve months, so this one runs to August 2027.
CH_SERVER_IMAGE=clickhouse/clickhouse-server:26.8.38.9

# f-infra-grafana. The ClickHouse plugin is downloaded once by
# `make grafana-plugin-prepare` and mounted in. Grafana itself is the
# official image and never reaches the internet at start-up.
GRAFANA_IMAGE=grafana/grafana:12.4.15
GRAFANA_CLICKHOUSE_PLUGIN_VERSION=4.23.5
TILESERVER_IMAGE=maptiler/tileserver-gl:v5.7.0
JAVA_JRE_IMAGE=eclipse-temurin:21-jre-noble
PLANETILER_JAR_URL=https://github.com/onthegomap/planetiler/releases/download/v0.8.4/planetiler.jar
# New York *State*, 498MB. The city is a small corner of it, but this is
# what the deployed map is built from, so it stays the default. BBBike's
# New York *City* extract is 153MB and covers everything this project
# renders - the dev profile uses it:
#   GEOFABRIK_NY_PBF_URL=https://download.geofabrik.de/north-america/us/new-york-latest.osm.pbf
GEOFABRIK_NY_PBF_URL=https://download.bbbike.org/osm/bbbike/NewYork/NewYork.osm.pbf
# Planetiler's JVM heap, and the hard ceiling on the container around it.
# Both are needed: -Xmx bounds the heap, --memory bounds everything else,
# and without the second an overshoot reaches the host's OOM-killer rather
# than the cgroup's. PLANETILER_MEM must stay above PLANETILER_HEAP.
# Deploy host: 8g heap in a 10g container, which is what the state file
# wanted. 3g of heap builds the city extract comfortably.
PLANETILER_HEAP=3g
PLANETILER_MEM=4g
# Host-side extras for Planetiler (not MapTiler). ~1.4G first fetch, then kept.
WATER_POLYGONS_URL=https://osmdata.openstreetmap.de/download/water-polygons-split-3857.zip
# Fallback only, for when the host above is down - it is the sole source of
# that file and has no mirror. `make tiles-water-from-image` repackages the
# copy baked into this image, which is the same ODbL dataset from the same
# origin, taken as a snapshot rather than fetched live. Pinned by digest
# because this image is still published; the snapshot inside this one is
# dated 2026-03-12. Never runs by itself.
WATER_POLYGONS_IMAGE=openmaptiles/import-data@sha256:8b48c4f7d68aac973e92523ca49862be7cb39febe78206c26b60504206e3656d
NATURAL_EARTH_URL=https://naciscdn.org/naturalearth/packages/natural_earth_vector.sqlite.zip
LAKE_CENTERLINES_URL=https://github.com/acalcutt/osm-lakelines/releases/download/latest/lake_centerline.shp.zip

# lion-prepare's throwaway gdal/psql image. Dated Debian snapshot, not
# debian:13-slim (a rolling suite tag).
DEBIAN_IMAGE=debian:trixie-20261104-slim


# =============================================================================
# 6. a-infra-postgres — the database
# =============================================================================
# One PostgreSQL. One listen. Debezium uses this port too.
PG_HOST=hailpath-postgres
PG_PORT=5432
PG_DATABASE=hailpath
PG_USER=postgres
# h-bootstrap's historical trips are routed through pgRouting,
# HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS (section 13) at a time - this pool needs at
# least that many connections plus headroom, or the extra workers just
# block waiting for one. Confirmed directly: left at 16 against 18
# workers, two workers sat permanently starved.
# 28 leaves the driver's 20 search threads a few connections to spare
# and stays under max_connections of 200 once every service
# has opened a pool.
#
# This one is shared by every service, and postgres.py opens a read pool
# AND a write pool of this size per process, so raising it to suit
# h-bootstrap alone would put the steady-state stack over max_connections.
# h-bootstrap has its own BOOTSTRAP_PG_POOL_SIZE below instead - it is the
# only thing that needs a large pool, and it runs while every service is
# still in standby.
PG_POOL_SIZE=28


# =============================================================================
# 7. b-infra-redis — the cache
# =============================================================================
# One Redis. Services use this hostname on the compose network.
REDIS_HOST=hailpath-redis
# Below the container limit on purpose: an AOF rewrite forks, and with no swap
# there is nothing to absorb the spike. Deploy host: 2560mb under REDIS_MEM=4g.
REDIS_MAXMEMORY=384mb


# =============================================================================
# 8. c-infra-kafka — the streaming backbone
# =============================================================================
# KRaft storage UUID. One broker still needs this — it is the identity
# formatted into the log directory, not a multi-broker cluster. Do not
# change it after hailpath-kafka-data exists.
KAFKA_CLUSTER_ID=2DgQxcGwSsWn1QTQzKeEFg
KAFKA_SASL_USERNAME=kafka
# The user inside the Kafka image, which the one-shot hands the volumes to.
# Confirm with:  docker run --rm apache/kafka:4.3.2 id
KAFKA_UID=1000
KAFKA_GID=1000
KAFKA_RETENTION_HOURS=48

# What an external client is told to dial on 9094. The advertised address
# is fixed at broker start. `make init` fills this from the default-route
# interface. Override it if the guess is wrong.
KAFKA_ADVERTISED_HOST=change-me-kafka-host

KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP=hailpath-kafka:9092
KAFKA_SECURITY_PROTOCOL=SASL_SSL
KAFKA_SASL_MECHANISM=PLAIN
KAFKA_SSL_CA=/etc/kafka/secrets/ca.crt
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_URL=http://hailpath-schema-registry:8081
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_KAFKASTORE_TIMEOUT_MS=5000
# The login a SQL client uses against ksqlDB. The password is
# KSQLDB_ADMIN_PASSWORD in section 1.
KSQLDB_ADMIN_USER=admin


# =============================================================================
# 9. d-infra-debezium — change data capture
# =============================================================================
# Debezium reads hailpath-postgres on PG_PORT.
CDC_PG_HOST=hailpath-postgres
CDC_PG_PORT=5432
CDC_PG_DATABASE=hailpath
CDC_PG_USER=postgres


# =============================================================================
# 10. e-infra-clickhouse — the analytics database
# =============================================================================
CH_USER=hailpath
CH_DATABASE=hailpath
# One ClickHouse. Clients use hailpath-clickhouse.
CH_HOST=hailpath-clickhouse
CH_HTTP_PORT=8123


# =============================================================================
# 11. f-infra-grafana — the dashboards
# =============================================================================
GRAFANA_ADMIN_USER=admin


# =============================================================================
# 12. THE SIMULATED CITY
# =============================================================================
# Nothing to set here: the city's zones are NYC TLC's own real Taxi Zones
# (TLC_ZONES_URL, section on the map, above), restored into city_zones by
# make lion-prepare/bootstrap the same way the routable graph is. bootstrap
# and every service that places anything on the map load that same table
# (hailpath_common.citygrid.CityGrid), so they always draw the same real city -
# there is no grid size or bounding box left to configure.


# =============================================================================
# 13. h-bootstrap — what gets created, once
# =============================================================================
# How much h-bootstrap may use. The routable graph is restored from an
# already-built dump now (h-bootstrap/lion-prepare/ does the heavy,
# memory-hungry topology-building step once, outside this container) - kept
# generous anyway, since too low a limit does not slow a step down, the
# kernel kills it outright and the run ends with "killed by SIGKILL". Raise
# this if that happens; it is transient, and the services are still in
# standby while it runs, so the memory is there to lend.
#
# Deploy host: 48g and BOOTSTRAP_CPUS=20. 48g was sized for a week of history
# at full scale; one day at 1/100 fits in 2g with room to spare. If it does
# not, the generator is holding something it should be streaming - worth
# finding out on a laptop rather than on a server where 48g hid it.
BOOTSTRAP_MEM=2g
# Matches PEOPLE_GENERATION_WORKERS (below): people.py's driver/passenger
# generation runs as that many real OS processes, pure CPU work - spawning
# more of them than this container is actually allowed to use just adds
# contention, not speed.
BOOTSTRAP_CPUS=6

# 1/100 of the deployment scale, holding the supply ratio exactly:
# 1060/4.55 = 232.97 = 106000/455, so dispatch sees the same pressure per
# driver as the real run. Same shape, same code paths, about 6,550 history
# trips instead of 4,401,860 - minutes instead of the 31-36 hours measured
# at full scale. A step that takes 31 hours cannot be developed against,
# only attempted, which is why step 13 has been attempted twice and
# finished zero times.
#
# Deploy host (Dionysus, 24 threads / 125 GiB): SEED_DRIVERS=106000,
# SEED_PASSENGERS=1500000, HISTORY_DAYS=7, HISTORY_TRIPS_PER_DAY=655000,
# TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=455.0.
#
# Earlier these were raised in h-bootstrap/.env.example and in every code
# default but missed here, the one file make init actually reads - .env
# silently kept the old numbers regardless of what the other copies said.
SEED_DRIVERS=1060
SEED_PASSENGERS=15000
# Generating a driver/passenger record is pure CPU work (Faker's name/
# phone/plate generation, mostly) - real OS processes, not threads, since
# threads would still serialize behind the GIL for this. Matches
# BOOTSTRAP_CPUS (section 13): more workers than the container's own quota
# just adds contention, not speed.
# Deploy host: 20 for each of the three worker counts in this section, and
# 24 for HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS. This host has 12 threads, not 24, and
# oversubscribing them does not go faster: every routing worker queues on the
# same Postgres, which is the bottleneck bench/route-bench.sql was written
# to time.
PEOPLE_GENERATION_WORKERS=4
HISTORY_DAYS=1
HISTORY_TRIPS_PER_DAY=6550
HISTORY_POSITIONS_PER_TRIP=8
# Deciding one historical trip's pickup/dropoff/outcome before routing is
# pure CPU work (zone-weight/OD-share lookups, distance decay - several
# per-zone loops, run once per trip) - real processes, same reason as
# PEOPLE_GENERATION_WORKERS above, and separate from it: this is bounded
# by history_days x trips_per_day, not driver/passenger counts.
HISTORY_GENERATION_WORKERS=4
# h-bootstrap's own pool size, overriding the shared PG_POOL_SIZE (section
# 6) for this one container. The routing pass checks out
# HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS connections at once and needs headroom above
# that: left at 16 against 18 workers, two workers sat permanently starved.
# Safe to make this large - nothing else is connected while bootstrap runs.
BOOTSTRAP_PG_POOL_SIZE=32
# Historical trips are routed for real, through the same pgRouting query
# dispatch-service uses live. This many route lookups run at once.
#
# Not a CPU count: these threads are blocked on Postgres, not computing.
# Measured on 2026-10-23 with PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP=20, 18 of these threads kept
# only about 14 of those 20 cores busy - each query spends roughly a fifth
# of its life waiting rather than running, so the pool was the limit and the
# quota was not. 24 fills the quota without oversubscribing it.
# BOOTSTRAP_PG_POOL_SIZE must stay above this.
HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS=6
# Real, road-snapped points precomputed per zone before assigning driver/
# passenger home locations and historical pickups/dropoffs - each point
# is a real database round trip, so this bounds that cost to
# zones x this number instead of once per driver/passenger/trip (up to
# ~1.6 million at full scale otherwise - confirmed directly, that was
# most of what made bootstrap's people-seeding step take tens of minutes
# with the host sitting idle in between calls).
ROAD_POINT_POOL_SIZE=300
# h-bootstrap uses HISTORY_ROUTING_WORKERS (above) to build its own pool,
# since it already needs that setting; driver-service and passenger-
# service build their own pool at their own startup too (hailpath_common.
# routing.build_road_point_pools, not just bootstrap's copy), and have no
# other reason to know about history's own worker count, hence this
# separate one.
ROAD_POINT_POOL_WORKERS=2
# How far hailpath_common.routing.nearest_road_point() will snap a candidate
# point to the road network before giving up and calling it off the map
# (open water, a park, a real island with no car bridge). Shared by
# h-bootstrap, driver-service and passenger-service - every place a
# pickup, dropoff or driver location gets picked.
MAX_SNAP_KM=0.5
# How far beyond the two points pgRouting is allowed to look. The edge list
# is read into pgRouting's own C++ memory in full before the search starts,
# so an unbounded list made every call read all 172k ways whatever the trip
# - on the 2026-10-23 full-scale run that held the week of history to 38.6
# trips a second. The box is the trip's own bounding box padded by whichever
# is larger: ROUTE_BBOX_MIN_PAD_KM, or ROUTE_BBOX_PAD_FACTOR of the
# straight-line distance. A box that finds no path is retried against the
# whole city before the pair is called unroutable, so "no path" keeps its
# meaning. The floor must stay comfortably above MAX_SNAP_KM, or a snapped
# endpoint could land outside the box the search was given.
# Set ROUTE_BBOX_PAD_FACTOR=0 to switch bounding off entirely.
ROUTE_BBOX_MIN_PAD_KM=3.0
ROUTE_BBOX_PAD_FACTOR=0.6

# The street map: NYC's own official LION data (DCP), not OpenStreetMap -
# real, DOT-verified one-way directions and posted speed limits, filtered to
# real drivable streets before anything else touches it. `make lion-fetch`
# downloads it once; `make lion-prepare` builds the routable graph from it
# once (both skip themselves when already done). Both are part of
# `make prepare`, so a normal deploy never thinks about either directly.
LION_URL=https://data.cityofnewyork.us/download/2v4z-66xt/application/zip
# The city's zones: NYC TLC's real, official Taxi Zones - the same 263
# neighborhood-shaped polygons Uber/Lyft/yellow cabs actually report trips
# against, not a synthetic rectangle grid. `make tlc-zones-fetch` downloads
# it once (part of `make prepare`, alongside lion-fetch); `make lion-prepare`
# builds city_zones_source from it in the same throwaway pass as the street
# graph.
TLC_ZONES_URL=https://data.cityofnewyork.us/api/geospatial/8meu-9t5y?method=export&format=GeoJSON
# One real month of TLC's High-Volume For-Hire Vehicle trip records, used to
# calibrate demand generation against real ride patterns instead of a flat
# random distribution - real busy zones/hours, real OD pairs, a real check
# on PLATFORM_COMMISSION_PCT. Pinned, not "the latest available": the same
# month always produces the same calibration, same reason RANDOM_SEED is
# pinned everywhere else. A few hundred MB, real billed traffic on a
# metered host - `make tlc-trips-fetch` downloads it once, on the host
# (part of `make prepare`, alongside lion-fetch/tlc-zones-fetch), and every
# zone-demand-prepare run after that reads the cached file instead of
# refetching it, including a retry after a failed run.
TLC_TRIP_DATA_MONTH=2025-01
TLC_TRIP_DATA_URL_TEMPLATE=https://d37ci6vzurychx.cloudfront.net/trip-data/fhvhv_tripdata_{month}.parquet
# The throwaway database `make lion-prepare` uses to build the graph. It
# exists for a few minutes on a private, stack-external network and is
# destroyed with the container - not a credential worth treating as one.
LION_PG_PASSWORD=throwaway-local-only
LION_PG_IMAGE=pgrouting/pgrouting:18-3.6-3.8.0
# How hard lion-pg is allowed to work while building the graph - tuned for
# speed, not durability, since the database is destroyed at the end of the
# same run. Sized for Dionysus (24 threads, 128GB RAM), this project's
# actual deployment target - override down for a smaller dev machine.
# Deploy host: 8GB/1GB/4GB/16GB and 24/16/8/8 workers, LION_PG_MEM=24g,
# LION_PG_CPUS=20, LION_PREPARE_MEM=4g.
LION_PG_SHARED_BUFFERS=2GB
LION_PG_WORK_MEM=256MB
LION_PG_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=1GB
LION_PG_MAX_WAL_SIZE=8GB
LION_PG_MAX_WORKER_PROCESSES=8
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS=6
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS_PER_GATHER=3
LION_PG_MAX_PARALLEL_MAINTENANCE_WORKERS=3
# How much of the host lion-pg/lion-prepare may use. Nothing else runs
# during this step (make prepare runs it before the real stack exists),
# so lion-pg gets the large share; lion-prepare is just a psql/ogr2ogr
# client waiting on it.
LION_PG_CPUS=6
LION_PREPARE_CPUS=2
# And how much memory. These are hard ceilings, not reservations: a
# container that reaches one is OOM-killed inside its own cgroup, so the
# host's own OOM-killer - which picks a victim anywhere on the machine -
# never fires. LION_PG_MEM must stay comfortably above
# LION_PG_SHARED_BUFFERS + LION_PG_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM; lower all three
# together on a smaller machine.
# lion-prepare builds the routing graph, and that work does NOT scale with
# the trip count - it is the same 172k LION ways either way, so it keeps the
# largest budget here. It runs with nothing else up, which is why it may be
# larger than any steady-state service above.
LION_PG_MEM=8g
LION_PREPARE_MEM=2g
# zone-demand-prepare reads the month in fixed 250k-row batches and keeps
# only running totals, so its memory does not scale with the month. 1280m
# against a measured 823 MiB peak on 2025-01's 20.4M trips. It held the
# whole month at once until 2026-11-10 and was OOM-killed inside 3g; see
# h-bootstrap/zone-demand-prepare/prepare.py for the three measurements.
# graph-restore is a pg_restore client, so the real work is in
# hailpath-postgres and its own ceiling.
ZONE_DEMAND_CPUS=4
ZONE_DEMAND_MEM=1280m
# Rows per batch. Lower it if a future month's records are much wider.
ZONE_DEMAND_BATCH_ROWS=250000
GRAPH_RESTORE_CPUS=2
GRAPH_RESTORE_MEM=1g
# Leave false. True skips restoring the graph, and routing does not work without it.
SKIP_MAP_IMPORT=false
# Run the seeding steps again on a database that already has data. Existing
# rows are still kept; this only lifts the "already prepared" short-circuit.
FORCE_RESEED=false


# =============================================================================
# 14. PRICES
# =============================================================================
# Read by bootstrap AND dispatch, so live trips are priced the way the seeded
# week was. Changing one without the other makes the history disagree with
# the present — which is why they are one setting here.
FARE_BASE=3.0
FARE_PER_KM=1.75
FARE_PER_MINUTE=0.45


# =============================================================================
# 15. PACING — the volume dials
# =============================================================================
# These decide how many messages a second the stack has to carry. If it
# struggles, turn these down; never remove containers. DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS
# and TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE are the two that matter most.

# j-service-driver — a position per online driver per tick.
DRIVER_TICK_SECONDS=3.0
DRIVER_SPEED_KMH=25.0
DRIVER_ONLINE_SHARE=0.6

# How many threads driver-service uses for pgRouting path building, both
# for the one-off "put the fleet on the road" phase at startup and for idle
# re-targeting every tick afterwards. 0 means follow PG_POOL_SIZE above,
# leaving four connections spare for everything else that reads.
#
# Why it exists: this was hardcoded at 4 against a PG_POOL_SIZE default of
# 5, while the deployed value is 24 - so it used a sixth of its own pool.
# Measured at the step-12 10% rung (10,600 drivers), the startup phase
# alone took 7m39s for 6,305 paths on four threads, inside a 17-minute
# startup during which the live marketplace produces nothing at all.
# Raise PG_POOL_SIZE with it, never past it.
# 20 threads run the paths the fleet actually needs at the same time.
# Four threads left the opening pass on a few cores for about an hour.
# The pool above is 28, so connections stay free for the other services.
DRIVER_ROUTING_WORKERS=4
# Extra idle drivers given a new street path every tick, on top of the
# ones who have actually arrived. 0 is the service: a driver is routed
# at startup and again when the current path ends. A positive number
# sends cars somewhere new early and exists only to keep Postgres hot.
DRIVER_STEADY_SEARCHES=0
DRIVER_SHIFT_CHANGE_CHANCE=0.01
DRIVER_DB_SYNC_SECONDS=30.0
DRIVER_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=60.0

# k-service-passenger — every request becomes a routing query in dispatch,
# which is the most expensive step anywhere in the pipeline. 455/minute is
# the real full-scale rate (~655,000 trips/day), not a dev-tier stand-in -
# same fix as SEED_DRIVERS/SEED_PASSENGERS above, this file had drifted
# back to the old 40.0 default.
PASSENGER_TICK_SECONDS=5.0
TRIP_REQUESTS_PER_MINUTE=4.55

# l-service-dispatch
DISPATCH_TICK_SECONDS=1.0
DISPATCH_SEARCH_RADIUS_KM=5.0
DISPATCH_MAX_REQUESTS_PER_TICK=10
# How many drivers dispatch will ask before giving up on a request. A
# driver may now decline or simply not answer, so a request is offered
# down a chain rather than assigned - this is how deep that chain goes.
# Five is deep enough that a thin zone produces real refusals instead of
# an instant no_driver_found, and shallow enough that a rider is not left
# waiting through five full 20-second timeouts in the worst case.
DISPATCH_MAX_OFFERS=5
# The speed behind the ETA a driver is shown at offer time. Deliberately a
# rough figure over straight-line distance rather than a real route: a
# pgRouting call per candidate would multiply dispatch's most expensive
# operation by the chain length, for a number that only has to be good
# enough to show and to predict acceptance.
DISPATCH_OFFER_SPEED_KMH=18.0
CANCEL_BY_DRIVER_CHANCE=0.06
CANCEL_BY_PASSENGER_CHANCE=0.07
TRIP_ACTIVE_TTL_SECONDS=7200
# The real driver-pay-to-fare ratio, measured directly against the same
# TLC calibration month (h-bootstrap/zone-demand-prepare) - see the
# commission-ratio line zone-demand-prepare logs when it runs.
PLATFORM_COMMISSION_PCT=0.23

# m-service-city
CITY_SCORE_SECONDS=30.0
CITY_TRAFFIC_UPDATE_MINUTES=5.0
# Six hours: the length of one part of the day, so a score cannot outlive
# the period it describes.
HOTSPOT_TTL_SECONDS=21600
# Read by city-service AND the sink, so "is this a hotspot" means the same
# thing to the one that writes the score and the one that files it.
HOTSPOT_SCORE_THRESHOLD=0.6

# n-service-clickhouse-sink — the time limit is what keeps dashboards close
# to live when the city is quiet and a batch would take minutes to fill.
SINK_BATCH_ROWS=1000
SINK_FLUSH_SECONDS=5.0
SINK_HOTSPOT_REFRESH_SECONDS=30.0
# How many recent event_ids the sink remembers, so a replayed batch is
# refused rather than written twice. Offsets are committed after a write,
# so a crash in between replays that batch; this only has to exceed what
# can be in flight, and 500k is far above it. ClickHouse will not do this
# for us - see n-service-clickhouse-sink/clickhouse_sink/seen.py.
SINK_SEEN_EVENTS=500000

# o-service-archiver — a trip is archived continuously in ClickHouse
# (trip_events, fed by clickhouse-sink from the same events every trip
# already publishes) well before it is 24 hours past ended_at, so the
# row is in the warehouse before it leaves Postgres.
ARCHIVER_TICK_MINUTES=15.0
ARCHIVER_RETENTION_HOURS=24
ARCHIVER_BATCH_SIZE=1000
ARCHIVER_MAX_BATCHES_PER_TICK=5

# i-service-cache-updater
CDC_TOPIC_PATTERN=^cdc\..*
CACHE_BATCH_SIZE=200
CACHE_FLUSH_SECONDS=2.0

# Same seed in all three generators, so a run can be repeated exactly.
RANDOM_SEED=20250824


# =============================================================================
# 16. KAFKA CONSUMER GROUPS
# =============================================================================
# One per service, and they MUST stay distinct. Two services sharing a group
# do not each get a copy of the stream — Kafka splits the partitions between
# them, so each silently receives a fraction of what it expects and drops the
# rest. That is why these are six separate names rather than one setting.
#
# Renaming one makes that service re-read from its topics' start.
CACHE_UPDATER_GROUP_ID=hailpath-cache-updater
DRIVER_GROUP_ID=hailpath-driver
PASSENGER_GROUP_ID=hailpath-passenger
DISPATCH_GROUP_ID=hailpath-dispatch
CITY_GROUP_ID=hailpath-city
SINK_GROUP_ID=hailpath-clickhouse-sink

# =============================================================================
# 17. RESOURCE LIMITS — cpus / memory per container
# =============================================================================
# These are sized for a 12-thread / 31 GiB workstation with no swap, and add
# up to 13.2 GiB of steady-state container memory. They used to be sized for
# the 24-thread / 125 GiB deploy host, which resolved to 54.6 GiB — more than
# a workstation has, and the failure was not a service refusing to start but
# the host OOM-killer picking a victim anywhere on the machine.
#
# The small scale is the default because it is the one that gets developed
# against. Each section below names the deploy-host values it replaced; set
# those on the server and leave them alone here.
#
# A ceiling never moves on its own. Every one of these containers has an
# INTERNAL ceiling sized against it — shared_buffers, max_server_memory_usage,
# each -Xmx, redis maxmemory — and the two have to change together. A 3g
# container running a Postgres that still wants 4GB of shared_buffers does not
# start; a JVM with -Xmx3500m in a 1280m cgroup is killed with nothing in its
# log to read. That is why those settings live here and not in the config files
# they used to be written into.
#
# `make stats` shows what each container is actually pressed against.

# --- a-infra-postgres --------------------------------------------------------
# PG_CPUS is what the one Postgres runs at after bootstrap.
# PG_CPUS_STEADY is the value `make pg-cpus-normal` writes back.
# PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP is the routing burst. h-bootstrap raises it before
# the history load and lowers it after a successful run. Recreating hailpath-postgres
# is required: a running container's cpus: cannot change without one.
#
# The burst must actually be a burst. Left equal to PG_CPUS_STEADY, the
# machinery still runs - pg-cpus-bootstrap seds the file and recreates the
# container - and writes 10 over 10, so the routing pass is capped at 10 of
# Dionysus's 24 threads through the most Postgres-bound phase of the whole
# deploy. Measured on the 2026-10-23 step-13 run: 21.2 routes/s, load
# average pinned at 10-11 on a 24-thread host with 104 GB free, ETA 57
# hours for the week of history. Nothing else is competing - every app
# service sits in standby until system:bootstrap:done is set, and the
# bootstrap container's own 18 routing threads are blocked on Postgres
# rather than using their BOOTSTRAP_CPUS. 20 leaves 4 threads for the host.
#
# Deploy host: PG_CPUS=10, PG_CPUS_STEADY=10, PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP=20, PG_MEM=16g,
# PG_SHARED_BUFFERS=4GB, PG_WORK_MEM=64MB, PG_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=1GB,
# PG_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=10GB, PG_MAX_CONNECTIONS=200, and the parallel
# counts at Postgres's own defaults of 8/8/2/2.
PG_CPUS=4
PG_CPUS_STEADY=4
PG_CPUS_BOOTSTRAP=6
# hailpath-postgres was OOM-killed near 8.3GB when the cap was 9g, with the host still
# having free memory.
#
# 3g is a floor set by the graph, not by the trip count. LION is all of New
# York at any scale - 172k ways - and pgRouting reads the whole edge list into
# its own memory on every call, so this cannot follow the simulation down.
PG_MEM=3g
# Sized against PG_MEM above and passed as `-c` overrides, so the ceiling and
# the settings that have to fit inside it move together. They were fixed in
# postgresql.conf until a 3g container met a 4GB shared_buffers and the server
# would not start. shared_buffers is the usual quarter of the container;
# effective_cache_size is a hint about the page cache this host can spare, not
# a reservation.
PG_SHARED_BUFFERS=768MB
PG_WORK_MEM=16MB
PG_MAINTENANCE_WORK_MEM=256MB
PG_EFFECTIVE_CACHE_SIZE=2GB
PG_MAX_CONNECTIONS=60
# Each parallel worker can claim its own work_mem, so these belong to the
# memory budget, not just the cpu one.
PG_MAX_WORKER_PROCESSES=6
PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS=4
PG_MAX_PARALLEL_WORKERS_PER_GATHER=2
PG_MAX_PARALLEL_MAINTENANCE_WORKERS=2

# --- b-infra-redis ------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploy host: REDIS_MEM=4g with REDIS_MAXMEMORY=2560mb (section 2).
# maxmemory is the dataset, the container is the process: 15000 passengers and
# 1060 drivers is a small fraction of what 384mb holds.
REDIS_CPUS=0.5
REDIS_MEM=640m

# --- c-infra-kafka --------------------------------------------------------------
# Every JVM in the stack: the heap and the container ceiling are two numbers
# and both have to move together. A heap larger than the cgroup it runs in is
# a container kill, not a Java OutOfMemoryError, so there is nothing in the
# logs to read afterwards. Keep each heap well under its *_MEM above - Kafka
# especially, which wants the remainder as page cache, and that is true at any
# scale.
#
# Deploy host: KAFKA_MEM=5.5g/-Xmx3500m, SCHEMA_REGISTRY_MEM=1g/-Xmx512m,
# KSQLDB_MEM=1.5g/-Xmx1g.
KAFKA_CPUS=1
KAFKA_MEM=1280m
KAFKA_HEAP_OPTS=-Xmx768m -Xms768m
KAFKA_DIRS_CPUS=0.25
KAFKA_DIRS_MEM=64m
KAFKA_CERTS_CPUS=0.25
KAFKA_CERTS_MEM=256m
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_CPUS=0.2
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_MEM=448m
SCHEMA_REGISTRY_HEAP_OPTS=-Xmx256m -Xms256m
KSQLDB_SECRETS_CPUS=0.25
KSQLDB_SECRETS_MEM=64m
KSQLDB_CPUS=0.5
# 768m rather than 640m: measured at 485 MiB resident while completely
# idle, which is 76% of 640m. The heap is only 384m of that - the rest is
# metaspace, code cache and direct buffers, and the direct buffers are
# exactly what grows once streams are actually running.
KSQLDB_MEM=768m
KSQLDB_HEAP_OPTS=-Xmx384m -Xms384m
KAFKA_TOPICS_CPUS=0.25
KAFKA_TOPICS_MEM=512m
SCHEMA_INIT_CPUS=0.25
SCHEMA_INIT_MEM=512m
KSQL_INIT_CPUS=0.25
KSQL_INIT_MEM=512m

# --- d-infra-debezium ------------------------------------------------------------
# Deploy host: DEBEZIUM_MEM=2g with -Xmx1g.
DEBEZIUM_CPUS=0.4
DEBEZIUM_MEM=640m
DEBEZIUM_HEAP_OPTS=-Xmx384m -Xms384m
CONNECTOR_REGISTER_CPUS=0.25
CONNECTOR_REGISTER_MEM=256m

# --- root (one-shots) -------------------------------------------------------
VOLUME_PERMS_CPUS=0.25
VOLUME_PERMS_MEM=64m

# --- e-infra-clickhouse -----------------------------------------------------
# Substituted into config.d/memory.xml and users.d/profiles.xml by ClickHouse's
# own from_env, in bytes. These were literals in those files, sized for an 8g
# container: lowering CH_MEM alone would leave a 6.5 GiB server ceiling inside
# a 2g cgroup, which is an OOM kill rather than a query that fails with a
# readable error.
#
# 1.2 GiB server-wide, 768 MiB per query, 128 MiB each cache. One developer
# reading dashboards does not need a gigabyte of mark cache.
# Deploy host: CH_MEM=8g, 6979321856 / 4294967296 / 1073741824 each cache,
# CH_MAX_THREADS=4, CH_CPUS=1.3.
CH_CPUS=1
CH_MEM=2g
CH_MAX_SERVER_MEMORY=1288490188
CH_MARK_CACHE_SIZE=134217728
CH_UNCOMPRESSED_CACHE_SIZE=134217728
CH_MAX_QUERY_MEMORY=805306368
CH_MAX_THREADS=2
CH_DDL_CPUS=0.25
CH_DDL_MEM=512m

# --- f-infra-grafana ---------------------------------------------------------
# Deploy host: GRAFANA_MEM=1g, TILES_MEM=3g/TILES_CPUS=1.0.
GRAFANA_CPUS=0.2
GRAFANA_MEM=384m
# hailpath-tiles had its ceilings only as compose defaults, so make init could
# not reconcile them and nothing in .env could override them.
TILES_CPUS=0.5
TILES_MEM=384m

# --- i through n — the six live services -------------------------------------
# Every one of these is a single Python process running one synchronous
# loop, no internal threading - a lone instance cannot use more than about
# one core's worth of Python bytecode no matter how high its own cpus
# ceiling goes; these numbers give the underlying C-level I/O (librdkafka's
# own threads, libpq) room to work beyond that, not a promise the Python
# loop itself will ever use all of it. Getting real use out of more than
# this per service means running more than one instance, which cache-
# updater and clickhouse-sink can already do (KAFKA_GROUP_ID is shared, and
# Kafka's own partition assignment splits the work) - see TODO.md.
#
# These follow the population down: 1060 drivers and 15000 passengers, not
# 106000 and 1500000. Deploy host: CACHE_UPDATER_MEM=1536m,
# DRIVER_SERVICE_MEM=2048m, PASSENGER_SERVICE_MEM=1536m,
# DISPATCH_SERVICE_MEM=1024m, CITY_SERVICE_MEM=1024m,
# CLICKHOUSE_SINK_MEM=2048m, and cpus of 1.5/2/1.5/1.5/1/2.5.
CACHE_UPDATER_CPUS=0.5
CACHE_UPDATER_MEM=448m
DRIVER_SERVICE_CPUS=1
DRIVER_SERVICE_MEM=512m
PASSENGER_SERVICE_CPUS=0.75
PASSENGER_SERVICE_MEM=448m
DISPATCH_SERVICE_CPUS=0.5
DISPATCH_SERVICE_MEM=320m
CITY_SERVICE_CPUS=0.5
CITY_SERVICE_MEM=320m
CLICKHOUSE_SINK_CPUS=0.75
CLICKHOUSE_SINK_MEM=512m
# archiver-service touches nothing but Postgres, in small batches, on a
# 15-minute clock - it never needs to be more than a fraction of a core.
ARCHIVER_SERVICE_CPUS=0.25
ARCHIVER_SERVICE_MEM=256m
